{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/178027-idea-mortal-communities-in-the-hedge","id":178027,"title":"[Idea] Mortal Communities In The Hedge?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Changeling: The Lost"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-06-23T09:44:37-07:00","id":178027,"title":"[Idea] Mortal Communities In The Hedge?","content":"<i>Consider the following:</i><br /> A mortal human is backpacking somewhere not too close to any large community. He's got all his gear with him, and the terrain he's exploring is beautiful-so breathtakingly beautiful, in fact, that it doesn't quite register to him when he walks through a hollowed-out old tree or disturbs the wrong fairy ring and winds up in the Hedge.<br /> <br /> Now, there are a lot of directions this could go-and the most likely ones in a story are probably &quot;the hiker is taken by an Other,&quot; &quot;the hiker eats a poisonous goblin fruit/meets an unfriendly hobgoblin and kicks the bucket,&quot; or &quot;the hiker runs across a group of player characters who rescue him.&quot; But what if none of those things happen? At some point, he'll have to realize he's lost, but maybe he's been going backpack-camping since he was 11 and decides that he's got nothing to worry about, he's been in this situation before and everything sorted itself out fine, and he'll just roll with it and set up camp somewhere around here (wherever <i>here </i>is...) The Hedge, responding to his quiet confidence, leads him to a relatively safe space, an uninhabited cave or a copse in the thorns with enough space to comfortably set up a tent and a fire for the night. Against all odds, he lasts the night, and the day after it too-maybe he eats the poisonous hedge-berries but yacks them an hour later and learns from his mistake, maybe a briarwolf does come after him but he brought a hatchet along and buries it right in the thing's snout, scaring it off.<br /> <br /> Point is, he makes it a lot longer than any of us would expect him to. Learns the lay of the land, to a certain extent. And at some point, he runs into a woman-not antlered or ablaze or with luminous skin like the only other things resembling people he's seen here (always from hiding) but a <i>normal </i>woman, one who looks normal and talks normal and has a normal reaction to all the abnormality of this place they've ended up in. She thinks she's been here for two days, and she's hungry, but he can show her at least a few edible plants, and she has a lighter which makes building a fire so much easier, and they band together out of both loneliness and necessity. And after that, maybe they find a third.<br /> <br /> It'll never be easy. The Hedge's effect on Morality means that such a group could easily descend into infighting and backstabbing. The Hedge mostly lacks electricity, limiting the technological scope of any settlement there. But for some, it's a worthwhile trade. Anarcho-primitivist idealists might rejoice seeing a chance to become entirely self-sufficient, removing themselves from the capitalist equation that is ubiquitous in the mortal world. Survivalists and paranoids see a possible new habitat for the remainder of humanity once the bombs have dropped or the sea levels have risen or whatever doomsday scenario they favor has come to pass. Creatives, the young, and the clinically depressed all might find relief in even the dangerously surreal aspect of the Hedge, liberated from the comforting boredom of a world that makes logical sense and placed in one of the fantastic ones they always dreamed of. Maybe they even learn how to work one or two gateways, and are able to send some members back to the mortal world to buy necessities otherwise unavailable to them (gas for a stove, batteries for flashlights, cooking supplies) and let them find their way back. Maybe they plant things and either learn to cultivate beneficial goblin fruits or unwittingly create a new breed when they attempt to transplant strawberries from someone's mortal garden. Maybe they bring in animals-some of those will die, and the ones that don't will of course change, but those will tend to remain friendly to the human settlement as long as none of its inhabitants freak out over the dog's coat turning the mottled green of the thorns and an extra tail dividing itself from the first one. Until an Other or a truly powerful Hobgoblin discovered it, a small community could potentially survive for a very long time in the Hedge.<br /> <br /> <br /> ...Now, at this point all I have is this fluffy stuff. How do you think you would incorporate such a community into a game of Changeling?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2709-quatrevingts","name":"QuatreVingts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2709_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"55"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-23T13:27:55-07:00","id":178184,"title":"","content":"Well ... if my memory serves me correctly, a normal dude who gets trapped on the other side of the Thorns is not in for a fun time. The psychoactive, soul shredding nature of the Hedge mutates and twists those creatures that are not properly abjured by the Wyrd. If the dude somehow manages to not get eaten or twisted by Arcadian mutations, I can imagine them becoming a changeling of a kind (maybe with the Fae touched merit, so they still look normal) or deteriorating into a hobgoblin! The Hedge is a horrifying place and it is governed by rules of cause and effect that work contrary to human perspectives, it isn't a place that normal peeps (even if they are creative) can find any kind of solace.<br /> <br /> But that being said ... let's say that due to some handwavium a human community somehow survives, untainted, in the depths of the Hedge ... I can imagine the community sorta devolving into a claustrophobic society ruled by bizarre superstition (which is the science of the Hedge's physics) and unyielding law. Like the Puritans. They would remain ever vigilant against any signs of witch's madness or fairy enchantments, and accusations of associating with goblins, changelings or fairies all bear the punishment of death by iron if they are found to have merit. They will undoubtedly be inbred. Have you seen that movie, The Village? Not that great of a movie, but good inspiration for this line of thought.<br /> <br /> Their extreme faephobia and unyielding conviction would make them a natural enemy of the freehold, but their isolation would make them a distant threat. Maybe, like the Gentry that they hate so much, the citizens of this Hedge town sneak out to the mortal world to kidnap an unsuspecting person every once in awhile in order to keep the &quot;iron in the blood.&quot; The younger the victim, the easier to indoctrinate into the town's bizarre culture ... ideally, they prefer infants. How do the Courts react when they learn that these &quot;Hedge-Men,&quot; not fairy lords and ladies, are responsible for the recent uptick in child abductions in the city?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1996-firefly-night","name":"Firefly Night","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"150"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-23T19:19:54-07:00","id":178528,"title":"","content":"^Ooooh, I LOVE that idea! As to the mutation issue, my fanon was always that animals and plants are susceptible to the transformative aspect of the Hedge in ways that humans wouldn't normally be, because animals/plants don't have souls. A human only starts undergoing the Changeling transformation once the Thorns have made a few cuts in their soul, allowing some of that Faerie magic to seep in.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2709-quatrevingts","name":"QuatreVingts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2709_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"55"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-23T19:23:34-07:00","id":178530,"title":"","content":"^Whoops-didn't mean to post quite yet and this forum does not appear to have a delete post button.<br /> <br /> The idea of such a community lasting multiple generations hadn't even occurred to me. I assumed it would be one or at most two generations, a few parents with children looking for &quot;a better world&quot; to raise their children in. Though, if one took it in that direction, I can see a minor template for children born to two normal parents in the Hedge-maybe similar to Cambions or whatever they were called, the children of Succubi?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2709-quatrevingts","name":"QuatreVingts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2709_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"55"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-27T03:16:59-07:00","id":181800,"title":"","content":"Yes, they are called Cambions.<br /> <br /> Well the situation I can see is for their to be refugees during a local apocalypse type situation: the Pompeii eruption, the tsunami that hit the Minoans, the disease that wiped out most of the Amerindians when the europeans came, etc.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/391-stsword","name":"StSword","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar391_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1635"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-04T09:53:14-07:00","id":188045,"title":"","content":"... and the refugees are <i>still there</i>. I like it.<br /> <br /> Honestly, I'm not really up on my Hedge lore, but I don't see why a mortal (or group of mortals) couldn't purchase a Hollow. Of course, they would need to constantly defend themselves against fae of both the lower- and upper-case-F sort; it wouldn't be easy. But it <i>would</i> be cool.<br /> <br /> Such a community would likely be insular, superstitious, paranoid ... and they would still lose children to the Others. Adults too. Might be a nice way to reiterate the central tropes the Lost evoke: Stay On The Path, trust no-one, don't provoke the Gentry, etc.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/877-mugasofer","name":"MugaSofer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar877_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"223"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-04T11:12:25-07:00","id":188120,"title":"","content":"But the very nature of the Hedge would warp those humans into something more... goblin sooner or later. At the very least, those communities should have developed some rituals/relics to delay the process, with variable rate of success.<br /> And I would love to see the Cambion minor template. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2411-raistlin","name":"Raistlin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"713"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-04T17:50:30-07:00","id":188336,"title":"","content":"There was a book of Plot Hooks called Glimpses of the Unknown, and one of the more interesting hooks for Changeling was the idea that a group of children had moved into the Hedge. Lost Boys, albeit more Lord of the Flies and less Peter Pan. And then it becomes a question of what happens <i>then</i>...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>GM of the <i>Walking Shadow</i> Campaigns</b><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/8992-the-hacks-and-houserules-of-walking-shadows\" target=\"_blank\">New System and Setting Material</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1040-neotiamat","name":"NeoTiamat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1040_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"471"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-05T05:08:01-07:00","id":188639,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Raistlin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/178027-idea-mortal-communities-in-the-hedge?p=188120#post188120\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But the very nature of the Hedge would warp those humans into something more... goblin sooner or later. At the very least, those communities should have developed some rituals/relics to delay the process, with variable rate of success.<br /> And I would love to see the Cambion minor template. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div> Well not really, while the hedges affect animals, plants, and even spirits, it apparently requires Arcadia itself to start mutating a person, and even changelings are 100% normal genetically, so humans really aren't affected like other things, because there's no way I believe that a tabby mutated into a shadow panther by the hedge is still a housecat genetically. <br /> <br /> However, I figure such a colony would probably require cultivation of hobgoblins and goblin fruit for long term survival. A 24/7 diet of such things could have some interesting results, supernatural merits might be extremely common for example, if the ST is up to it perhaps they have sorcery based on changeling contracts, or can turn secrets into one time supernatural powers like that optional rule if the autumn court is ascendant.<br /> <br /> Oh and Cambions are in Dancers in the Dusk I believe.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/391-stsword","name":"StSword","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar391_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1635"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-08T02:12:31-07:00","id":191249,"title":"","content":"I remember a section in Mortal Remains that states that while extremely unlikely it is possible, given enough time, research, and willpower, for a mortal to enter Arcadia. If that's possible I see no reason for your hypothetical Hedge Village to be any less possible. Although there's also a moniker to the aforementioned section saying that while possible the mortals in question would still have to deal with the consequences of going to Arcadia, and so too would your &quot;Hedge People&quot; have to deal with the consequences of living in the Hedge for years on end. <br /> <br /> If I were to use your Hedge Village in a Chronicle I'd have the player's Motley stubble upon it purely by the grace of the Wyrd after a particularly violent confrontation with a rival Motley or Privateers. The settlement would be styled after the one from The Beach, another terrible movie yet a surprisingly accurate example of what you're essentially describing, wherein the humans inhabit a pristine Hollow where they've created their own version of paradise but are driven to drastic means of keeping their &quot;Paradise&quot; a secret for their own protection. The players would be wounded from their previous fight and now stuck in the middle of hostile territory, surrounded by normal humans that are convinced they're Hedge Beasts or the Others. To make matters worse the players would be the unwitting catalyst for the Village, after years of having their Morality slowly degrade as they ignorantly indulged in their Vices, to finally erupt into chaos as long building tension is finally given a means of release. Add a dash of panic as the ensuing chaos alerts a nearby True Fae to the Villages presence and the Motley will have one heck of a challenge, both physically and morally, to overcome. After all, anything is better than going back to Arcadia...right?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3436-dusksage","name":"Dusksage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3436_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"869"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-08T04:35:24-07:00","id":191300,"title":"","content":"I think most communities would try to struck a bargain with the local hobs or even a True Fae for protection, if they want to survive. About the price... well, I guess for a Keeper is handy to have a &quot;supermarket&quot; in the Hedge rather than going to the Human World for new toys. And the goblins may want some &quot;sacrifices&quot;as merchandise.. or food<br /> Can anyone post the Cambion lesser template?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2411-raistlin","name":"Raistlin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"713"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}